# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
QUIZ = {"09": [
    ("Why can a venous needle dislodgement go unnoticed by the machine?",
     ["The measured venous pressure is mostly needle and line resistance, so it may not fall enough to alarm", "The machine turns off alarms during dialysis", "Dislodgement always triggers the air detector", "Blood loss is always slow"], 0,
     "Best: the venous pressure alarm can stay silent when the needle is out. Why not the others: alarms stay on; the air detector does not detect dislodgement; loss can be rapid."),
    ("You find blood under a blanket and the venous needle out. What are the first steps?",
     ["Stop the pump, clamp both sides of the break, and press firmly on the site", "Reinsert the needle", "Return the blood through the open line", "Increase the pump speed"], 0,
     "Best: these stop the loss and control bleeding (Card 4). Why not the others: reinsertion and returning through an open end risk infection and air; pump speed worsens loss."),
    ("What is the danger of returning circuit blood after hemolysis?",
     ["It carries potassium released from broken red cells", "It causes air embolism", "It has too much heparin", "It is too cold"], 0,
     "Best: hemolyzed blood carries potassium and is never returned (Card 6). Why not the others: those are not the reason."),
    ("One patient has port-wine blood in the lines. What should the charge nurse do next?",
     ["Check every patient on the same water and concentrate supply and consider a cluster", "Restart that patient on a new dialyzer", "Return the blood slowly", "Wait for the next shift"], 0,
     "Best: hemolysis is often a supply problem affecting several patients. Why not the others: restarting before the cause is found is unsafe; the blood is never returned; waiting endangers others."),
    ("Within five minutes of starting dialysis, a patient develops hives, wheeze and low pressure. What is first-line treatment?",
     ["Intramuscular epinephrine into the outer thigh as the protocol directs, with the pump stopped and blood not returned", "An antihistamine and observation", "A steroid IV and continue dialysis", "Return the blood and finish the session"], 0,
     "Best: epinephrine IM comes first; antihistamines and steroids do not replace it; the circuit blood carries the trigger (Card 7). Why not the others: delay epinephrine or return the trigger."),
    ("What is the key difference between anaphylaxis epinephrine and cardiac arrest epinephrine?",
     ["IM 1 mg/mL for anaphylaxis with a pulse versus IV 0.1 mg/mL (1 mg) for arrest", "They are the same dose and route", "Arrest epinephrine is given IM", "Anaphylaxis epinephrine is always IV bolus"], 0,
     "Best: route and concentration differ; mixing them up causes tenfold errors. Why not the others: they misstate routes or concentrations."),
    ("A patient arrests from anaphylaxis. What does AHA 2025 say about fluids?",
     ["Isotonic IV fluids are reasonable (COR 2a, LOE C-EO)", "Fluids are contraindicated", "Only colloids may be used", "Fluids are given only after ROSC"], 0,
     "Best: fluid resuscitation is reasonable in anaphylactic arrest. Why not the others: none matches the guideline."),
    ("A dialysis patient on an opioid has slow, shallow breathing, pinpoint pupils and a pulse. What comes first?",
     ["Ventilate with a bag-mask and give an opioid antagonist per protocol", "Start chest compressions", "Wait for the drug to wear off", "Give atropine"], 0,
     "Best: respiratory arrest with a pulse needs ventilation; an antagonist should be given for suspected opioid overdose. Why not the others: there is a pulse; waiting risks arrest; atropine does not reverse opioids."),
    ("In suspected opioid-associated cardiac arrest, when may naloxone be given?",
     ["When it does not interrupt high-quality CPR", "Before starting compressions", "Instead of epinephrine", "Only after ROSC"], 0,
     "Best: an antagonist may be reasonable in arrest from presumed overdose if it does not interrupt CPR. Why not the others: compressions come first; it does not replace the arrest algorithm; it is not restricted to after ROSC."),
    ("A diabetic patient becomes confused and sweaty in the third hour of dialysis. What is an early check?",
     ["Capillary glucose", "Dialysis fluid conductivity only", "A chest X-ray", "Serum calcium only"], 0,
     "Best: low glucose is a common, treatable cause of confusion on dialysis (Card 11). Why not the others: they may matter later but do not catch the most likely treatable cause quickly."),
]}
